                "title": "Italic style used",
                "description": "Text is formatted using CSS italics.",
                "complementary": "If this styling is intended to add meaning, be aware that it probably won't be conveyed to assistive technologies.\n##### Recommendation\nIf the italics are intended to convey meaning, such as emphasis or a citation, then the appropriate semantic elements should be used, such as `<em>` or `<cite>`. Using inline semantics is the most future-proof way of conveying such meaning. However note that most current screen readers don't actually describe any semantics for these elements, nor will they necessarily change their speech output to convey the italics (such as by voice stress). This aspect of speech output is determined by user settings and internal heuristics, and cannot be controlled by authors.\n##### Resources\n* [Accessibility: Bold vs. Strong and Italic vs. Emphasis](https://help.siteimprove.com/support/solutions/articles/80000448460-accessibility-bold-vs-strong-and-italic-vs-emphasis)\n* [Screen Readers support for text level HTML semantics](https://www.tpgi.com/screen-readers-support-for-text-level-html-semantics/)",
