                "complementary": "If this element is intended to add meaning, be aware that it probably won't be conveyed to assistive technologies.\n##### Recommendation\nIf the intention is to convey meaning with italics, such as emphasis or a citation, then the appropriate semantic elements should be used, such as `<em>` or `<cite>`. Using inline semantics is the most future-proof way of conveying such meaning. The `<i>` element is only suitable for outlying cases where italics are a typographical convention, such as idiomatic phrases or a speaker's thoughts. However note that most current screen readers don't actually describe any semantics for any of these elements, nor will they necessarily change their speech output to convey the italics (such as by voice stress). This aspect of speech output is determined by user settings and internal heuristics, and cannot be controlled by authors.\n##### Resources\n* [Accessibility: Bold vs. Strong and Italic vs. Emphasis](https://help.siteimprove.com/support/solutions/articles/80000448460-accessibility-bold-vs-strong-and-italic-vs-emphasis)\n* [Screen Readers support for text level HTML semantics](https://www.tpgi.com/screen-readers-support-for-text-level-html-semantics/)",
